{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Level bias (most horizons): model is systematically low vs realized prices during rising regimes; apply an upward offset of ~+1.0 to +2.5 EUR for days 1–20 when the last 5–10 days show an uptrend (positive slope).",
    "Underreaction to bullish breakouts: when spot jumps >+2 EUR in 1–3 days, the next 5–15 day path is typically higher than forecast by ~+1–3 EUR; add a breakout premium that decays with horizon.",
    "Late-horizon crash/mean-reversion overshoot: the model often predicts an excessive sell-off in days ~20–30 (notably after strong rallies), ending 3–8 EUR below realized; dampen late-horizon drawdowns by pulling days 20–30 toward the 10–15 day forecast level (e.g., shrink negative drift by ~50–70%).",
    "Trend-lag: forecasts appear to lag regime shifts; increase responsiveness by blending in recent momentum (e.g., add ~0.3–0.5 × 5-day return to days 1–10, tapering to 0 by day 20).",
    "Overconfident upside spikes at long horizon: occasionally the model overshoots high levels at days ~25–30 (e.g., projecting >70 when realized ~66–69); cap long-horizon excursions by mean-reverting extremes toward the 20-day realized/expected range (soft cap ~+3 EUR above recent 20-day average)."
  ]
}
